I have had a frustrating time teaching pets to steal from shops.  It
is hard to get the treat to them when they've dropped the item just in
front of the door but before they move away.  When it works, boy does
it work; but I rarely get it to work and usually resort to "follow me
up the hallway," getting between him and the door, and waiting for the
drop.

Today though I have a bell, and I'm ringing it to call him down the
hallway.  It's working well, but I'm thinking that it would be nice,
and realistic, to train him with food away from the shop and then just
ring the bell for him to steal stuff for me.  Pseudocode for this
would be:

o - Add a PAVLOV variable that tells how much the pet associates a
calling instrument (bell, tin whistle) with food.
o - Whenever the pet eats food that has been in the PC's inventory,
increment PAVLOV if the PC applies the calling tool within a certain
amount of time.
o - Whenever the PC applies such a tool, make a check of PAVLOV, and
if it passes, do whatever it is that successful training results in.
o - Maybe decrement PAVLOV occasionally by turncount, much less than
it increments with food.  Maybe also decrement it when you ring and
don't give it food.

Is this worth implementing?  If so, how?



